#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342214 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4abe11e-febf-5461-96ee-bca8a7713bdf" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Definite Integral of a Piecewise Linear Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e11e6707-d2e2-5fee-afc6-46454c46a61f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342216} {:answer/id #uuid "a3973676-5332-57e8-a724-95d70a4ba495" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342217}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a3973676-5332-57e8-a724-95d70a4ba495" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342217} :answer-field/id #uuid "0867d975-b31a-5413-b1c9-724054d513ae" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342215}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "701dff71-e2c9-5a39-9c92-e7e127a3d233" :question/math-academy-id "q-316132" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061763/assets/b871a949beaac0319d77364c43aa261bda8c28642f8bfc80a80f974fd7323cf3.png)\n\nThe graph $y=f(x),$ shown above, consists of two line segments. Then,\n\n${\\int }_{-2}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We divide the interval of integration into $3$ regions: the line segment for $x\\in [-2,-1],$ the line segment for $x\\in [-1,1),$ and the line segment for $x\\in [1,3].$ Therefore, we can write the required integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-2}^{-1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{-1}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{2}}+{{{\\int }_{1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{3}}.\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-2,-1].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}(bh) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-1,1).$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}(bh) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{2}=-2.\n$$\n\n- The $3$rd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [1,3].$ The corresponding region is a square, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & ={s}^{2} \\\\  & ={2}^{2} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}-{A}_{2}+{A}_{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}-2+4 \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293780 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb30dbbf-4f83-59d0-b712-f0e592aafb27" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Definite Integral of a Piecewise Curved Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c18aa5b7-c992-5b32-a357-5e724ba96bd5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9+\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293782}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c18aa5b7-c992-5b32-a357-5e724ba96bd5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9+\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293782} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4170770-80a0-5c01-a8a3-394cfa36e747" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293781}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f14565cc-b995-5571-b40e-c5bb6b6d3c35" :question/math-academy-id "q-316261" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061763/assets/d36f2859865da58d226a738d8338f726df950db75b7421b604bb692c9049d54a.png)\n\nIf the graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above, then\n\n${\\int }_{-4}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The graph consists of $2$ parts: the arc of the semi-circle for $x\\in [-4,-2]$ and the line segment for $x\\in (-2,1].$ So, we can use the additive property of integrals to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-4}^{-2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{-2}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-4,-2].$ The corresponding region is a semi-circle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi \\cdot (1)^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (-2,1].$ The corresponding region is a trapezoid, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}(a+b)h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2+1)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\frac{9}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi +9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293783 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4abe11e-febf-5461-96ee-bca8a7713bdf" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Definite Integral of a Piecewise Linear Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d0bf7010-3f64-5b22-8fc4-82fc0116c4ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293785} {:answer/id #uuid "eabd0215-ed94-5435-bebd-84d0f8f97cc6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "17.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293786}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d0bf7010-3f64-5b22-8fc4-82fc0116c4ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293785} :answer-field/id #uuid "603a54c5-8911-5f08-b53b-a8d967ee08be" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293784}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "550da44a-dc56-593e-bbdf-dd12d1acd2aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-338507" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061763/assets/8eb7c4fe147028c6dc31aad2427a33bb713553e610101b1fd263bda30f5c49d0.png)\n\nThe graph $y=f(x),$ shown above, consists of three line segments. Then,\n\n${\\int }_{-6}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We divide the interval of integration into $4$ regions: the line segment for $x\\in [-6,-4],$ the line segment for $x\\in [-4,-3),$ the line segment for $x\\in [-3,0),$ and the line segment for $x\\in [0,5].$ Therefore, we can write the required integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-6}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-6}^{-4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{-4}^{-3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}+{{{\\int }_{-3}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{3}}+{{{\\int }_{0}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{4}}.\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-6,-4].$   The corresponding region is a triangle below the $x$-axis, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}(bh) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-6}^{-4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{1}=-2.\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-4,-3).$   The corresponding region is a triangle, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}(bh) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $3$rd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-3,0).$   The corresponding region is a rectangle, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & =bh \\\\  & =3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $4$th integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [0,5].$   The corresponding region is a trapezoid, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{4} & =\\frac{1}{2}(a+b)h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3+1)(5) \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-6}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-{A}_{1}+{A}_{2}+{A}_{3}+{A}_{4} \\\\  & =-2+\\frac{1}{2}+6+10 \\\\  & =\\frac{29}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342218 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb30dbbf-4f83-59d0-b712-f0e592aafb27" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Definite Integral of a Piecewise Curved Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7b03183e-f29b-5823-8cd8-e02d3a36e2b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9\\pi}{4}+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342220}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7b03183e-f29b-5823-8cd8-e02d3a36e2b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9\\pi}{4}+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342220} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4b917c2-594f-5e61-a022-6fcda75fcdb8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342219}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9e602f85-6313-5b1e-91a0-e275215905a2" :question/math-academy-id "q-338511" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061763/assets/def5dbb4e5a338159e09c098e47e0b78cbe59d31bcd67478f4dbb7b1c85488f9.png)\n\nIf the graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above, then\n\n${\\int }_{-2}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The graph consists of $2$ parts: the arc of the quarter circle for $x\\in [-2,1)$ and the line segment for $x\\in [1,5].$ So, we can use the additive property of integrals to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-2}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{1}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-2,1).$ The corresponding region is a quarter circle of radius $3,$ so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\pi \\cdot (3)^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [1,5].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}2\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\pi }{4}+4 \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\pi +16}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342221 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb30dbbf-4f83-59d0-b712-f0e592aafb27" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Definite Integral of a Piecewise Curved Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e9fbfef-1271-5f1b-9d8a-ff0eb8a31264" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pi+7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342223}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5e9fbfef-1271-5f1b-9d8a-ff0eb8a31264" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pi+7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342223} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1f94c02-3c0b-5f46-a640-b274177f4906" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342222}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "080d27d4-373a-55c7-9b81-6a1bc5f1e92a" :question/math-academy-id "q-339679" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061763/assets/38270fdcb312cdce6a76e1d88bfa0117b4a69b5024b2acfd0456646471ead71f.png)\n\nIf the graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above, then\n\n${\\int }_{-6}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The graph consists of $4$ parts: the arc of the quarter-circle for \n$x\\in [-6,-4),$ the line segment for $x\\in (-4,-1],$ the line segment for \n$x\\in (-1,1],$ and the arc of the quarter-circle for $x\\in [1,5].$ \nTherefore, we write\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-6}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-6}^{-4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{-4}^{-1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}+{{{\\int }_{-1}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{3}}+{{{\\int }_{1}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{4}}.\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the\n$x$-axis, where $x\\in [-6,-4).$ The corresponding region is a rectangle minus a quarter-circle of radius $2,$ so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =bh-\\frac{1}{4}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =(2)(2)-\\frac{1}{4}\\pi (2)^{2} \\\\  & =4-\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-6}^{-4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{1}=\\pi -4.\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (-4,-1].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}(bh) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $3$rd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (-1,1].$ The corresponding region is a trapezoid, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}(a+b)h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3+1)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $4$th integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [1,5].$ The corresponding region is a combination of a semi-circle of radius $2$ and a rectangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{4} & =bh+\\frac{1}{2}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =1(4)+\\frac{1}{2}\\pi (2)^{2} \\\\  & =4+2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-6}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-{A}_{1}+{A}_{2}+{A}_{3}+{A}_{4} \\\\  & =\\pi -4+3+4+4+2\\pi  \\\\  & =7+3\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
